WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:40.180
[Narrator:] Patient Wilfred Ayers talks to surgeon B. D. Monroe.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.830
[Dr. Monroe:] So Mr. Ayers, if this proves to be a cancer of the lung,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:48.130
your outlook or your prognosis

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:53.690
is almost 100 percent nil for any length of time,

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.360
and with operation and complete removal of the tumor

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:02.170
and the tumor-bearing area, you have a reasonably good outlook.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.220
But then once it's completed,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.740
the only thing to do is to go back to your job and live with optimism.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.220
[Mr. Ayers:] And quit smoking.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.950
[Dr. Monroe:] And quit smoking.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.400
[Narrator:] Wilfred Ayers will walk these streets again,

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:17.750
but minus the cigarettes and minus a lung.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.550
In a week he will have a major operation,

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.020
which we will see, and which may save his life.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.870
Last year in North America, 50,000 people died

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.480
from cancer of the lung,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.260
a disease which has become a major killer

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.060
only since the smoking of cigarettes has become epidemic.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.080
Many more thousands died of coronary heart disease

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.240
or were disabled by chronic bronchitis, emphysema,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.520
and other illnesses which medical men the world over

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.780
say can be related directly to the smoking of cigarettes.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.070
Almost a hundred and fifty years ago Charles Lamb wrote,

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:53.270
"For thy sake tobacco, I would do anything but die."

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.670
Medical men say too many people are doing just that.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.770
[Suited man:] In view of his history,

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:14.470
in view of his history of heavy smoking

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.700
for a goodly number of years would you care

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.430
to give any opinion as to the relationship

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:25.230
between his smoking and the fact that he appears now with

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:30.000
proven bronchogenic carcinoma of the squamous cell variety?

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.120
[Doctor 1:] I definitely feel that it did have a relation to the smoking,

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.110
but I don't think we can prove it.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:37.940
[Suited man:] I think there is no question

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:45.580
that prolonged cigarette smoking is responsible,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.630
I presume, for carcinoma.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.520
Certainly these people get carcinoma

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.750
and the logical assumption is the responsibility of the cigarettes.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.260
Now what in the cigarette smoking is responsible for it,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.670
I don't think we can say at all.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:06.440
Whether or not it's tar or some other product of combustion

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.960
is not at all clear,

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.090
but I think the association is clear,

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:17.670
and I think that it is certainly more than adequate to be acted upon

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.790
in, in advising people

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.380
whether or not to smoke.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:25.800
[Second suited man:] And you will be the first one, I'm sure will agree,

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:28.080
that there are other deleterious effects of

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:33.740
smoking that perhaps haven't got the fatality of a cancer of the lung,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.020
but from an economic point of view

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.390
and from the point of view of disabling a person,

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:40.180
they are equally as important.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.600
And I'm talking particularly about

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:45.140
the chronic bronchitic and the emphysematic people.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:51.170
[Suited man:] Coronary artery disease seems to have some association.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:58.610
Certainly there are some cases where this appears to be clear-cut.

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:02.580
There is a time-honored association with Buerger's Disease...

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.920
there is no doubt that there are a number

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.360
of areas of medicine where you can get

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:16.110
this deleterious association between disease and smoking.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.650
[Mr. Ayers:] I've done the stupidest thing,

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.470
I've smoked cigarettes before break..

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:22.690
before having anything,

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.880
three and four cigarettes as soon as I get out of bed

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:29.240
which I think is uh, no good to anybody.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:30.890
And my wife said to me several times,

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.330
she said, "Why ever don't you stop smoking with that cough?"

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.390
[Dr. Monroe:] You've always had a cough?

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:39.440
[Mr. Ayers:] I always found that I coughed quite heavily

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.400
after I had a smoke,

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.290
because I always inhale my smoke.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.520
[Dr. Monroe:] Yes. What was the symptom or the reason

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.720
that motivated you to seek the advice of a doctor in the first place?

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:53.100
[Mr. Ayers:] Well, for the past nine months

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.500
when I went to see Dr. Halley I had a streak of blood on my phlegm.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:58.340
[Dr. Monroe:] In your sputum?

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.230
[Mr. Ayers:] My sputum. So I thought well,

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:02.810
this has gone far enough,

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.260
and my wife said why don't you go and see the doctor?

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:06.350
So I said I will.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:07.920
I'll make up my mind one of these days.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.860
I didn't know what I was involving myself in.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:11.880
[Dr. Monroe:] When was that? This is important.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:15.700
[Mr. Ayers:] I should say about, uhh, nine months ago.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:18.190
[Dr. Monroe:] And when did you go to the doctor?

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.180
[Mr. Ayers:] It was, uh, a month ago.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.090
[Dr. Monroe:] Just a month ago.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.180
So you waited eight months.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.160
[Mr. Ayers:] I waited eight months.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:24.250
[Dr. Monroe:] Why?

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.250
[Mr. Ayers:] Well, I thought it was a blood vessel

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.920
I'd broken when I coughed but it wasn't.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.530
[Dr. Monroe:] I put these x-rays up here,

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:34.630
not specifically for you to completely understand them

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.210
but to give you a rough idea of what your problem is.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:40.640
Now if you look at this x-ray here,

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:44.570
you'll see that you have a shadow which is generalized

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.340
in the upper part of the left lung,

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.470
and if you compare this to the right lung,

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.380
you'll see that there's a difference

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.060
in the degree of penetration on both sides.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.070
What this represents is that you have a complete obstruction

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:02.060
to the upper lobe of your left lung.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.530
Now let me be very frank with you, Mr. Ayers,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:08.290
the thing that we're all concerned about this day

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:13.000
is the fact that this could very likely be a tumor

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.290
that's obstructing your airway.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.570
This tumor is undoubtedly likely to be a malignant tumor.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.460
In other words, it could be a cancer.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.180
And if it proves to be a cancer the only satisfactory treatment

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.710
is to remove it completely.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:31.590
Uhh, this will mean that you will lose your left lung.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.430
[Mr. Ayers:] And the other lung is good, isn't it?

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.480
[Dr. Monroe:] Oh as far as we know it's an excellent lung.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.600
[Mr. Ayers:] Excellent condition,

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:43.070
so that is one reason why I think that the left lung should come out.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:44.950
Little did I know 'til I came in here

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.140
that I had only a lung and a half or less.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:47.920
[Dr. Monroe:] All right, Mr. Ayers,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.740
we'll carry on with the bronchoscopic examination tomorrow,

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:51.790
and after that there will be

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.540
no further delay.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.240
If it's indicated we'll take you to surgery.

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:02.380
Now Mr. Ayers, we are going to carry out this bronchoscopy procedure

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:05.490
that I explained to you yesterday.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.100
And this is the instrument.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:09.560
The light is on the end and it's hollow.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.390
We put this through your air tree

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:14.430
and this is just a simple diagram of your air tree

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.230
with the lung at the side.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:17.740
Pay no attention to this side.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.910
This is your left lung,

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.320
and this is the part that is involved.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.000
In other words, this part of your lung,

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.180
which is your upper lobe,

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.880
is collapsed because there's something

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.380
plugging the airway going into it.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.710
Right here you have a bird's eye view,

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:35.570
so to speak,

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.210
of the air tree as we look sideways

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.460
through a special little instrument that's actually

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.220
a miniature periscope that we can look at a right angle.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:50.300
And these openings here which feed air into this upper lobe are plugged,

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.150
and the object of this is to put down the bronchoscope--

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:56.210
we turn your head to the side to line it up--

00:07:56.230 --> 00:07:57.470
and we have it lying in here,

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:01.150
the main airway going through the entire left lung.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:05.770
And then we look sideways into this left upper lobe bronchus,

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.940
and we observe directly with the human eye

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:12.650
exactly what the condition and the nature of that air tube is.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.640
And this is the object of the examination.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.220
I don't think it'll bother you very much,

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.250
we'll do it under local anesthesia,

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:19.570
we'll freeze your throat

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.430
and uh I think you'll find it very easy.

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.330
[Mr. Ayers:] Okay then, Dr. Monroe.

00:08:24.350 --> 00:08:26.710
[Anesthesiologist:] Your tongue will get stiff

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.670
and it'll be a little difficult to swallow for you,

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:34.550
so that will be an indication that you start to get frozen.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:42.600
[Mr. Ayers inhales the solution with deep breaths.]

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.530
[Dr. Monroe:] Okay, that's fine.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:50.750
We are ready to uh, just lie down sir.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:51.940
All right, thank you.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.980
Just raise your head sir, raise your head.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:00.330
Now put it down.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:03.450
I don't think you'll find this examination will upset at all,

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:06.960
so just uh, what size is this? Number seven?

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:08.380
[Nurse:] Eight.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.050
[Dr. Monroe:] Eight, that's fine.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.630
Just stick out your tongue, sir.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.550
Now just relax.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:17.370
Nice and easy.

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:21.750
Nice and easy.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:23.700
That's it.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.860
No problem there at all.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:32.490
[?] can I have the [?] scope please?

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.380
All right.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:41.360
Now we are right in your main airway now, Mr. Ayers

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:44.610
and we're looking down into your main airway,

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:48.610
and we can see the two divisions.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:53.380
Now we have these little telescopic instruments

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.180
that I was talking to you about

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.100
and we've got this through the hollow tube

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.330
and we're looking on the interior of your airway,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.310
and we can see it very nicely.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.950
I'll have a right-angle scope now.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:09.460
And now we're going to put in the telescope

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.500
that's a miniature periscope so we can look sideways.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:22.540
And with this instrument we're able to see side branches directly.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.920
You're doing very well, Mr. Ayers.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:29.000
There's no problem here at all and we'll soon be finished.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:34.540
All right, Mr. Ayers, we're all finished. Just sit up, sir.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.530
I'll take this off your head.

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:42.090
Just swing your feet over the side.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.840
Now did we bother you very much?

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:49.600
[Mr. Ayers:] No, Dr. Monroe, I... no bothering, no bothering,

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:52.650
no trouble, I could feel very little of it.

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:55.090
[Dr. Monroe:] Well, we've determined that your right lung

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.240
looks very clear and your left,

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.200
the main part of your left airway is fine,

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:05.170
and that our suspicions that the left upper lobe airway was blocked

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.030
has been confirmed.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:12.840
And uh, so this is plugged, and it's plugged by a tumor growth.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:16.100
So our next job now is uh to fix you up

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:18.510
and to get rid of this and uh

00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.590
we'll proceed with that next week.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:22.800
[Mr. Ayers:] Sure.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:23.890
[Dr. Monroe:] Okay, Mr. Ayers.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:28.330
[Mr. Ayers:] Thank you, Dr. Monroe.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.760
[Coughing sounds]

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:41.570
[Narrator:] Not all smokers have the reasons to stop that Wilfred Ayers has.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:45.960
But not all who develop cancer have the warnings he had either.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.750
Cancer is a silent killer and more often than not

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.290
does not proclaim its onset.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:56.400
And doctors now urge that those who smoke have x-rays twice a year.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.390
Too many smokers come with their problems too late,

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:03.190
when surgery or radiation or other treatments cannot cure them.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:10.530
[Man in hospital coughs violently.]

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:11.800
Breathing tests help determine

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:14.190
how he will function on only one lung.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.860
[Nurse:] Deep breath and blow.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:17.050
[Mr. Ayers:] And if I ring the bell

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.300
you're going to give me a cigar, right?

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.950
[Nurse:] Or force you to stop smoking.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:25.830
Blow. Keep blowing, keep blowing, keep blowing as much as you can.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.600
[Physician:] I'm really interested in his oxygen uptake.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:30.650
This is really what I'm...vis a vis one lung against the other.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:31.750
Easy.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.320
[Narrator:] The capacity of each separate lung

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.050
can be plotted on a spirometer.

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.740
[Physician:] That's all you need calculated, sure.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:41.450
We just want one against the other anyway.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:42.590
[?] increase after.

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:46.900
[Technician:] I think we'd better give him some more Demerol, don't you?

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:51.150
[Physician:] This is the moment of truth.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:54.100
That's a bang-on, you know.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:56.240
That thing's in there real tight.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:59.310
I mean it's a good differential spirometry.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:02.280
[Head Doctor speaking to seated group of medical staff:] Physical examination was essentially unremarkable

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.240
apart from the fact that there was gross abnormality

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:09.190
in the respir.. respiratory sounds in his left upper chest,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:12.680
suggesting a collapse of his left upper lobe.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:15.950
The rest of the physical examination was negative

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:18.900
and the test x-ray was grossly abnormal.

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.250
He's had extensive investigations,

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.870
many of which are of little interest because they're essentially

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:29.710
normal including electrocardiograph and his pulmonary function studies.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:31.050
[Suited man:] Has he lost any weight?

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:33.190
Does he have any constitutional symptoms?

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.270
[Head Doctor:] He's had no weight loss at all, sir.

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:36.330
[Doctor 1:] When I first saw the patient,

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:37.890
he was an extremely healthy-looking man.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:40.840
I think almost clinically you could tell that

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:42.600
he wouldn't present a risk to surgery,

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:46.110
and I think it's his only chance for, uh,

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:49.240
possible cure of his disease.

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.410
[Head Doctor:] He has no evidence of cardiovascular disease?

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:52.990
[Doctor 1:] None whatsoever.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.240
[Audience member:] May we see the x-rays?

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:58.630
[Radiologist presenting x-rays:] Now, the [?] findings are such

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:05.540
that they indicate an obstruction in the left upper lobe bronchus.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.140
[Doctor 1:] Would you call the remainder of his lung normal?

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.380
[Radiologist:] The remainder of his left lung is normal.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:16.960
His right lung shows no evidence of any disease.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:20.060
[Doctor with dark hair, standing up:] We took a biopsy from this tumor mass

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:22.630
and Dr. Bellin can tell us about that.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:25.510
[Dr. Bellin:] Yes, the biopsy showed it's squamous cell carcinoma,

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:27.060
there's no doubt about it.

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:30.910
[Voice from audience:] And no uh symptomatology elsewhere

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:33.000
that would suggest extra duress?

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.630
[Dr. Bellin:] No, none at all, sir.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:37.830
[Suited man:] These pulmonary function studies will be more helpful

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:41.180
in making sure that he has enough reserve to

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:43.620
withstand this sort of surgery.

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:45.380
Is that how you got the...

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.720
[Dark-haired doctor:] Well, sir, he had lung capacity done,

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:50.520
maximum breathing capacity, timed vital capacity,

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:53.010
and pulmonary diffusing capacity,

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:55.000
and with carbon monoxide uptake.

00:14:55.020 --> 00:15:00.060
The opinion of the uh pulmonary function consultant uh was,

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.120
in summary, the findings reported above

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.690
are reasonably good for a male in this age group.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:07.180
[Doctor 1:] And the flow rates are perhaps a little bit down.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:08.680
Not as much as I would expect

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:11.360
with his long history of smoking.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.670
His diffusing capacity was normal.

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:16.660
I wouldn't consider these a contraindication

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:21.130
to a pneumonectomy.

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.340
[Suited man:] Dr. Pollack?

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:25.540
[Dr. Pollack:] I agree with Dr. Monroe

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.200
and with the general course of the discussion

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.590
and I think in this case,

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:32.840
operation is the best way to proceed.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:34.640
[Nurse:] As a result of the anesthetic,

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:36.570
you pile up secretions in your chest

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:38.610
and it's difficult for you to cough,

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:42.760
and the breathing exercises can make this simpler for you.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.470
So let's just check them again.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:45.410
[Mr. Ayers:] All right.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.850
[Nurse:] You don't have to fear coughing after the operation, Mr. Ayers,

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:51.190
because I'll be supporting your incisions.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:57.190
So I'd just like to practice again with the breathing in and out.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:00.320
Short breath in, long breath out.

00:16:00.340 --> 00:16:06.160
And cough.. [Mr. Ayers coughs] in and again.

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.170
[Narrator:] There is little hope among medical men

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.240
that those who now smoke will stop,

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:14.720
despite new medical knowledge that much lung damage can be reversed by stopping.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.430
They only hope that examples like Mr. Ayers

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:23.470
will cause non-smokers not to begin.

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.000
[Nurse:] Good morning, Mr. Ayers.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.500
How do you feel about the surgery now?

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:29.940
[Mr. Ayers:] Oh I uhh, the way I look at it is,

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:35.970
it's a must, through what Dr. Monroe and other doctors have told me.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.610
And uhh I put myself into their hands completely.

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:42.360
I mean he told me once, that he said he,

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.200
a little while back there that he didn't give

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:48.210
too much chance for my life, you see,

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.170
if I didn't have something done.

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:52.520
I've heard that there is lots of uh individuals

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.220
going around with one lung.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:58.280
As a matter of fact there is a lady I know very well through uh,

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:01.450
daily contact sometimes in a store you see,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:03.240
and she only has one lung.

00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:06.540
And I think that you can't tell any difference

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:09.130
whether she's got one or a half a dozen.

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:13.600
[Nurse:] We'd like to recommend that you try to rest as much as you can,

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.650
and to relax as much as you can.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:20.570
[Mr. Ayers:] When I start to come to from the anesthetic, you call it?

00:17:20.590 --> 00:17:21.420
[Nurse:] Yes.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.750
[Mr. Ayers:] Umm, am I likely to get into a tantrum,

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:25.520
will I get mad?

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.300
[Nurse:] No, no, no you will wake...

00:17:27.320 --> 00:17:29.500
[Mr. Ayers:] I had that experience once.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:33.750
They uh plugged the ether right about here.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:37.770
and I was asleep at seven o'clock, came round about midnight the same..

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:40.330
I was wounded you know from [?]

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.000
and it took about twelve of 'em to...

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.390
and I was cursing everybody around me you know.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:46.800
[Mr. Ayers chuckles.]

00:17:46.820 --> 00:17:49.570
It wasn't funny...

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:52.150
[Nurse:] Yes, well, Mr. Ayers, there is nothing to that.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.750
[Mr. Ayers:] I just had my little doubts

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:57.810
because I wouldn't want to uh cause any commotion like that you know.

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:00.910
[Nurse:] No, no there's no fear of that, no.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:03.950
[Mr. Ayers:] In my.. I'm not going to uhh say that

00:18:03.970 --> 00:18:09.680
it's actually done uhhh by smoking, you know what I mean,

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:12.260
I've left it to the doctors to say,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:17.460
but when I was told not to smoke, I had not coughed.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:23.300
And I should have taken my dearly beloved wife's advice years ago

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:26.080
and cut down on the smoking you know.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.860
But sometimes you get into a little bit of a uhh,

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:33.190
sometimes you, I mean you get into a jam

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:35.970
and the first thing you do is pick up a cigarette

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:38.290
and you keep smoking and smoking and smoking you know.

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.410
[Nurse:] Um hmmm.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:42.610
[Mr. Ayers:] When you're told you that have only got...

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:46.420
that the prospects in life is not very far ahead of you, you know,

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:50.000
then you sit up and take notice, because life is sweet.

00:18:50.020 --> 00:18:57.390
[Anesthesiologist:] Blood pressure is 130.

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:59.940
Mr. Ayers, we are injecting the pentothal

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:02.000
in the intravenous now,

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:03.960
and you will just fall off to sleep

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:06.970
very quietly, very pleasantly.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:11.640
You may notice a little peculiar taste or smell,

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:16.990
something like onions or garlic.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:20.610
[Dr. Monroe:] We are now ventilating only the right lung.

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:26.410
The left lung is excluded from the airway.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:38.520
[Anesthesiologist:] Blood pressure is 120.

00:19:38.550 --> 00:19:42.990
Now, we let the respirator, the automatic respirator,

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:47.930
take over control of ventilation.

00:19:47.950 --> 00:19:55.430
Now we're ready to position the patient for the surgery.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:59.290
[Dr. Monroe:] Help position his legs properly, please.

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:06.590
[Anesthesiologist:] Heart rate has speeded up now.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:16.760
[Dr. Monroe:] All right, now uh, we're about to make the incision.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:26.380
Dr. Brendel, is everything all right?

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:31.340
This particular patient, we'll undoubtedly take out a rib

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:33.590
to facilitate the surgery.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:39.010
[Dr. Brendel, anesthesiologist:] Blood pressure reading is 90.

00:20:39.030 --> 00:20:45.870
[Assisting surgeon:] Can I hold this for you?

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.120
[Dr. Monroe:] Now up to the second rib.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.920
That's two, that's three, that's four, that's five.

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:02.660
This rib is very high up.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:13.560
[Surgeons work together to remove a rib in order to get to the left lung.]

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:31.210
[Surgeons murmuring.]

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:37.940
[Dr. Monroe:] This is something...we'll get it out.

00:21:37.960 --> 00:22:00.560
[Surgeons talk amongst themselves as they deal with complications.]

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:08.110
[Dr. Monroe:] Can you hold that [?] over...

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:18.980
[More talking and murmuring.]

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:24.940
Right there.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.620
It's going to come.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.220
It's going to come but it's not uh,

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:38.020
it's not the most favorable situation.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:41.110
[Words difficult to discern because of surgical masks over faces.]

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:43.220
The [?] doesn't seem to be...

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.310
[Dr. Monroe:] The [?] is clear.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:46.270
[Assisting surgeon:] It's not involved.

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:51.290
[Dr. Monroe:] And there's nothing in the mediastinal area up above.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:52.370
[Assisting surgeon:] It seems to be mainly the pulmonary artery

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:56.810
and maybe the superior [?] vein.

00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:58.540
Well, we'll just have to work away

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:05.060
until we get the thing cleared out.

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:11.460
All right, we're just about through.

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:14.720
Just about ready to sever the lung.

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:17.710
Now what I have in my hand here is just the air tube

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:19.160
and the other air tube along going to the other

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.130
lung branch is right there.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.250
We're going to put a clamp across it right here,

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:25.260
This is a soft clamp.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:28.060
It's gentle to the tissues

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:32.390
and we'll clamp the air tube like so.

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:33.740
Now just give me a knife please.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:38.720
I think we should set that a little further down.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:42.110
We'll sever the connections here.

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:47.930
And as we do Dr. [?] keeps cutting it out.

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:50.170
And there we are.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:56.260
This is what we've been after this morning is to remove this lung

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:59.880
containing the tumor mass which I have in my fingers here.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:02.260
Dr. Bieland will take this and demonstrate

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:04.610
how all the big vessels,

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:06.140
and everything that's come off the heart,

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.220
they're all tied off here.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.750
This is an important side of course,

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:16.550
this side is next to the heart.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:30.540
[Assisting surgeon:] Now let's [?] the lung completely,

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:33.110
so that we can have a good view

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:37.830
of this neoplasm or tumor.

00:24:37.850 --> 00:24:39.880
We start from the upper lobe,

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:44.520
and we'll go down to the base of the lung.

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:48.860
Here is the tumor in the upper lobe,

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.560
occupying almost half of it.

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:53.650
It's not a very large tumor,

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.700
just I would say medium-size.

00:24:56.720 --> 00:25:01.470
It is a hard white mass as you can see here.

00:25:01.490 --> 00:25:04.660
The rest of the tissue is soft and spongy

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:07.170
and this tumor is very hard

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:10.480
It is fairly well demarcated

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:13.400
and the definite prognosis can be established only

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:15.820
after microscopic examination.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:19.290
[Nurse:] How do you feel now?

00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:21.430
[Mr. Ayers:] Oh, not too bad.

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:24.090
[Nurse:] Not too bad. The operation's all over.

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:25.770
[Mr. Ayers:] Thank you.

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:28.930
[Nurse:] Try to keep your eyes open.

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:31.290
Now will you take a deep breath for us?

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:33.230
[Mr. Ayers makes raspy breathing sounds.]

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:42.390
Deep, and out, and in, out...

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.760
That's it.

00:25:44.780 --> 00:25:47.820
You're trying to give us a smile now?

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:50.160
[Mr. Ayers:] Sure.

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.790
[Narrator:] Wilfred Ayers has opened his eyes again on a world

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:55.750
that wears a cigarette in its face.

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.490
Where he will have to live with temptations

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:01.140
that confront a smoker wherever he looks and listens.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:04.140
Where persuasion filters out of the mind of the beholder

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.210
the consciousness of the harms that medicine

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:09.510
now definitely attributes to cigarettes.

00:26:09.530 --> 00:26:14.350
A world whose governments are split between the revenues they gain from cigarettes

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:18.280
and the responsibility they feel for the health of their peoples.

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:21.130
A world that seems to have no ready answer to the question

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.790
of how far laws should go in curbing the

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:26.600
rights of people to make their own choices

00:26:26.620 --> 00:26:29.190
and take their own consequences.

00:26:29.210 --> 00:26:31.980
A world where the young are taking ever earlier

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:34.190
to a habit that public health associations

00:26:34.210 --> 00:26:37.000
have estimated will claim the lives of millions

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:38.740
who are now schoolchildren

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:45.500
before they reach the age of 70.

